WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:11.190
DAVID FEUER: Hello, all.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.300
And welcome to Dev 302, Building
and Managing GraphQL APIs.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.567
I'm Dave Feuer, and I'm
joined by Greg Brail.

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:18.900
We're a part of the Apigee team.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.612
And we're super excited to
present this to you today.

00:00:21.612 --> 00:00:22.820
GREGORY BRAIL: Hey, I'm Greg.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:24.230
I'm also part of
the Apigee team.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.105
And I'm also excited.

00:00:26.837 --> 00:00:29.170
DAVID FEUER: I guess the first
thing I wanted to get out

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.630
of the way is that
sometimes, in technology,

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:36.330
we can overemphasize how new
technologies and exciting ideas

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.720
are dominant in
the conversation.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.650
And so I just wanted to be
clear-- and I think both Greg

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:44.800
and I share-- that REST
is still the king of APIs.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.010
If you look at this graph,
which is from Google Trends,

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:51.100
it clearly shows that REST
is still the king of APIs

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.850
and still dominates interest.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:55.120
And so while we're here to
talk to you about GraphQL

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.190
and some other API
styles, it's important

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:00.160
that we realize that REST still
has a very important and very

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.560
prominent position in
building and designing

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.710
APIs for the future.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:08.470
If we're going to contrast
GraphQL and REST APIs,

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.630
I think there's three
specific areas that we'd

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.200
want to talk to you about.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.700
The first is that both GraphQL
and REST offer a consistent way

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.870
to access different
types of data.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.160
So while GraphQL and REST
construct things differently

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.410
and construct requests
differently and get responses

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:27.370
differently, fundamentally, they
both allow you to access data.

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.800
GraphQL offers a query
language that's different,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.140
of course, than the
REST query style.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.080
And that can potentially work
across many types of back ends.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.720
But fundamentally, they're
both requesting data,

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.140
and they're both offering
a consistent access

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.650
pattern for the data.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.530
The second is efficiency.

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.830
While it's possible to construct
REST APIs in an efficient way,

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.770
GraphQL is specifically
built to avoid

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.780
over-fetching or
under-fetching data.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.760
The query language
is super powerful

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.950
and allows you to
request just the data you

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.710
need for your client, whether
it's a mobile client, a web

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.350
client, or another
style of application.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:05.170
And the last is
implementation complexity.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:08.229
Implementing an HTTP server
to parse URLs and return

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:11.643
data from a variety of sources,
that's a straightforward task.

00:02:11.643 --> 00:02:13.810
And many people do that in
their environments today.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.200
But GraphQL requires specific
software and a specific engine

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.510
on the server side that
can parse the queries

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.550
and break them down
into execution steps.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.440
And so that's a
significant difference

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.980
between GraphQL and REST.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.770
To recap some of the
benefits of GraphQL,

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.010
I think data load control
and granularity are

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.190
super important.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.640
The fact that GraphQL
has a single endpoint

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.850
and allows you to really
execute one request

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:42.490
and then have the fan-out
on the southbound side

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.350
make a whole bunch of
subsequent requests

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.120
and return that, it's
very, very powerful.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.300
It also means that if
one resolves or fails,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.940
other queries can still
succeed and still resolve

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:53.380
and bring back useful data.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.900
In the REST world, REST doesn't
necessarily work that way.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.483
REST has multiple
different endpoints.

00:02:57.483 --> 00:02:59.530
And so each query
to each endpoint

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.575
has to be treated individually.

00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:03.700
GREGORY BRAIL: So could
you maybe talk a little bit

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.270
about, as a software engineer,
maybe I'm building an app.

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:11.800
Why would I choose GraphQL,
and why would I choose REST?

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.520
DAVID FEUER: Great,
great question.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.490
I think there's a lot
of apps-- specifically,

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.880
apps built using the ReactJS
framework-- that really benefit

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.800
from the fact that
GraphQL will only

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.690
get the data that's in view.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.070
So that for
instance, if you were

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.320
tracking sports
statistics, you only

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.270
retrieve the sports
statistics for the player

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.130
that you're looking
at, or you only

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.620
receive the sports statistics
for the specific table

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.450
that you can view
in a mobile app.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:36.280
As opposed to REST,
which might require

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.320
additional APIs or
additional parameters

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.330
to accommodate all
the different views

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.380
that might exist in an app
and create individual APIs

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.670
for those different views.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.370
So being able to leverage
the GraphQL query

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.400
language to really only
get the data that you need

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.430
for that specific user creates
a certain amount of efficiency

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.860
and a certain amount of
developer safety, where

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.410
they know they're getting the
data that they expect when they

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.780
expect it, versus having to
make multiple calls in order

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:04.810
to fill out whatever
table the user

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.025
might choose to view
in that application.

00:04:07.025 --> 00:04:07.900
Does that make sense?

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:08.733
GREGORY BRAIL: Yeah.

00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:11.890
So with REST, I'm used to using
an API gateway like Apigee,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:16.000
where I might have a couple of
customized versions of my API

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.920
for different devices
because I want

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.290
to have it be more efficient.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:22.900
Are you saying, with
GraphQL, I have more options,

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:24.940
I have more
flexibility in the data

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.340
that I return to my client?

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.340
DAVID FEUER: Absolutely.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.040
Along with the control
that gives you the ability

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.430
to request exactly what you want
and retrieve exactly the data

00:04:33.430 --> 00:04:35.960
you want also comes a tremendous
amount of responsibility,

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:36.460
right?

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:39.220
Suddenly, you have to figure
out up front what data you want,

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.560
versus simply calling an API.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.540
That being said,
with that control

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.970
comes a certain amount of
developer safety, as we said,

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.250
where calling that
one API just becomes

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:49.660
very simple and very easy.

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:51.310
And that query
language is really

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.460
what provides that control
in the GraphQL world.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:55.960
GREGORY BRAIL: So
a mobile developer

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.670
might really like
the fact that they

00:04:57.670 --> 00:04:59.450
can choose exactly
what they want,

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:02.960
send one request to the server,
and get it all back at once,

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.190
rather than having to navigate a
whole forest of individual URLs

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:08.830
in order to get the
same information.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.000
DAVID FEUER: Absolutely.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.920
Yeah, that's a
great point, Greg.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.740
The third point is that
whereas in the past,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.778
GraphQL required a whole
bunch of specific glue

00:05:17.778 --> 00:05:19.570
in order to work with
different programming

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.028
languages and
different frameworks,

00:05:21.028 --> 00:05:23.560
we're really seeing a
proliferation of GraphQL client

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:24.190
libraries.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.570
So GraphQL really is available
as a polyglot protocol,

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:32.410
and is no longer simply
reduced to a few languages

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.300
or a few frameworks.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.300
GraphQL is really fairly
open and really portable.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:42.010
And so we're seeing a change,
a shift, in adoption of GraphQL

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.470
as a result of that.

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.370
So I think if we
take a step back,

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:51.120
we'll see that there are
really two communities of use

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.170
for APIs.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.790
There are API
programs, which are

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.760
tight-knit teams where
the client and server are

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:57.540
tight-knit group.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.082
Or sometimes, they're
the same person

00:05:59.082 --> 00:06:01.050
that's developing both
the server application

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.875
and the client application.

00:06:02.875 --> 00:06:04.500
And then, there are
developer programs,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.500
which are really about
creating APIs as products,

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.240
and exposing those APIs to
those different communities

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.050
so that different developers
can build their own front

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.730
ends of their own
application servers

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:18.450
that leverage your APIs
to create different value

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:19.390
propositions.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.070
So in that specific instance,
it could be a different business

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:22.570
unit.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:23.610
It could be a different company.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.690
It can be a partner.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.490
But you no longer
have the same scope

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.790
of control over both the
server and the client.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:34.210
And that's a significant change.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.170
But that's a significant
difference between API programs

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.780
and developer programs,
that in API programs,

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.640
you really don't have
to be as strict about

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:42.660
design of your API.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.180
Because since you control
the client and the server,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.300
you can assume a certain
amount of knowledge,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.197
and mediate between
that on your own.

00:06:50.197 --> 00:06:51.780
But when you have a
developer program,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.310
the clients don't
know what you know.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.435
And so you have to make
sure to actually productize

00:06:55.435 --> 00:06:57.120
your API in such
a way that makes

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.400
it discoverable and
accessible and makes

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.170
it easy for
developers to leverage

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.330
and consume that API
in order to drive

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:07.120
business value not just for you,
but also for their application.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:10.490
GREGORY BRAIL: So if
I'm using GraphQL,

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.200
how do I know a
developer is not going

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.290
to do something crazy
and query a bunch of data

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.255
they aren't supposed to get?

00:07:17.255 --> 00:07:18.130
DAVID FEUER: Exactly.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.390
Greg, perfect setup.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:22.720
I think that's the fundamental
challenge, is that we don't.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.000
We don't know what
clients are going to do.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.488
In REST, we know
what APIs we expose.

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:29.530
And so we know what clients
are going to request,

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.210
because that's what the API is.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:33.580
The API exposes that resource.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.160
In GraphQL, when you're
exposing all of your resources,

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.620
like a graph, clients can really
request whatever they want.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.820
And so we're seeing GraphQL
struggling in that area,

00:07:42.820 --> 00:07:45.310
in the area of developer
programs, where it's been super

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.350
popular with API programs.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:50.710
And I think what we're doing
with Apigee is super exciting

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.080
here, in taking all the
learnings that we've

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:55.810
had from productization
of APIs and bringing them

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.160
over to GraphQL.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.840
Greg, why don't you
talk a little bit

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:02.650
about API productization and how
we're bringing that to GraphQL?

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.520
GREGORY BRAIL: Awesome, thanks.

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.770
So if we look at
this chart, this

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:11.580
talks about a lot of the
things that an API team

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.230
needs to think
about when they're

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.630
trying to turn an
API into a product,

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:19.260
taking something that's just
an HTTP endpoint with some JSON

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:21.120
and turn it into
something that developers

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.370
from different organizations
and different companies

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.510
can consume.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.040
And a lot of these
things are things

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.740
that if you've been following
the world of API management

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.200
or seen in the
Apigee things before,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.049
you've probably heard of
about a lot of these things.

00:08:34.049 --> 00:08:36.179
Timing to First Hello,
World, for instance--

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.220
that's what that
acronym is for--

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.860
is a really important
metric for an API program

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.650
if you want developers
to adopt your API.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.520
Having a community, having
standards, like oAuth,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:51.450
as part of your API, and having
things like developer portals.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.270
And this enables, really, one
of the most important things

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.580
that's a difference
between an API

00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:00.480
used by a small team and a
developer program used by lots

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.790
of developers and
different organizations,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.040
is the ability to
do self-service.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.960
And when we talk about
API productization,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.100
a big part of it
is self service.

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:11.970
How does a developer who
wants to use your API

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.630
find out about the API,
figure out what the API does,

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:16.860
and get credentials?

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:19.170
If they can do that
in a few minutes,

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:20.730
your API is ready
for consumption

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.330
by almost anyone in the world.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:23.760
And that applies
whether your API

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.900
is a REST API of the style we've
been talking about for years

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:31.380
and years in the world of API
management or a GraphQL API.

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:38.390
Now, when we talk about this,
we talk about control, security,

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:40.520
observability, and governance.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.650
And in the world of
REST APIs, we often

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.010
do a lot of these things based
on the fundamental principles

00:09:46.010 --> 00:09:48.830
of REST, like URLs and verbs.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.410
We'll do things like,
we'll say, if you've

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.990
got credentials for
the API, you are

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:57.770
authorized to invoke certain
URLs, but not other URLs.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.720
Or perhaps you're authorized
to get all the URLs,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.170
but if you want to
post some of the URLs,

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:07.520
you need a different
kind of credentials.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:10.728
All of these same things are
going to apply to GraphQL.

00:10:10.728 --> 00:10:12.770
We're going to ask a lot
of these same questions.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.770
So when David talked about
making a GraphQL API ready

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:18.377
for consumption by
developers, the developers

00:10:18.377 --> 00:10:20.210
will love it, because
now I can do anything.

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:22.430
I have access to
your whole schema.

00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:23.880
I can query all kinds of stuff.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.100
And I can build a great app.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:27.830
And what I've said for years
and years about this stuff

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:31.310
is that it's not just that you
have to worry about an attacker

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:33.122
or someone who's malicious.

00:10:33.122 --> 00:10:34.580
You also have to
worry about people

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:36.680
who just make mistakes, right?

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:40.250
Like, oh, I didn't realize
that by executing that GraphQL

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:44.120
query, I would download 10,000
rows of data from the database

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:47.690
and clog up the internet and
make my mobile device crash.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:48.650
Now, I know that.

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:50.630
So I know I have to
include a parameter.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:53.870
But wouldn't it be nice if
we could put some controls

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:57.200
in front of the GraphQL API,
so a developer can't even

00:10:57.200 --> 00:10:58.625
do that sort of
thing by accident?

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:05.360
So what we're
doing at Apigee is,

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:08.000
we're starting on a
journey of figuring out

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:10.850
what we need to
do to make GraphQL

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:15.350
APIs the same kind of
first-class APIs as the REST

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.920
APIs and the other kinds of
APIs that Apigee traditionally

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.190
supports.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.640
So in the traditional
world of API management,

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:27.320
we have this chart where we
have API teams build APIs,

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:31.340
developers build apps for APIs
to create user experiences

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.140
for end users.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:37.430
And that transition from
an API used by an API team

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:40.710
to a developer program is
what this is all about.

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:44.280
The question is, when we
are working with GraphQL,

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:48.000
how does this differ from the
way it looks for a REST API?

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.160
And the answer
is, not very much.

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.410
All of the same
principles apply.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.570
The developer wants
to be able to discover

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:56.400
the API via self-service.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:58.140
They want to get credentials.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.820
They want to use the API.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:02.580
The API team wants
to have control

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.680
over who's allowed
to use their API

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.170
and how much they're
allowed to use the API.

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:09.630
Are they allowed to use
different parts of the API

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.010
for different purposes?

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:14.910
Is there a quota that stops them
from using the API too much?

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.980
Are there security
rules or audit rules?

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:20.280
Is there even a billing plan
associated with that API?

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.470
We want to be able to
do all of these things

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:26.520
for GraphQL APIs, which means
that we have to not only look

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:29.520
at URLs and verbs, but we
have to look at the structure

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.810
of the GraphQL query.

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.390
And we have to understand,
when you make a query, what

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:34.750
is this query trying to do?

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.500
And as an API team,
what kind of things

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.630
can I let the developers do?

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:42.360
And you can do this by having
lots of different GraphQL

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.260
endpoints.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.020
But one of the advantages
of an API management system

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.720
is that it allows
the API team to be

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:52.150
very sophisticated in turning
those APIs into products.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.260
So if they want to launch with
an API that does five things

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.620
and sell it to customers for
a certain price or for free.

00:12:58.620 --> 00:13:00.540
But then, they want to
add two more things,

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:02.040
but they only want
certain customers

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:03.902
to be able to do those things.

00:13:03.902 --> 00:13:05.610
And then, they want
to add one more thing

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.200
that only a few
customers can see,

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.030
but no one else
should find out about.

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:12.660
You can do all of this in
your API management product

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:14.610
without changing
your API at all.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:16.170
You do this by restricting.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.750
In Apigee, you do this
by creating something,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:21.120
something called an API product.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.310
And an API product
basically specifies

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.340
which parts of the API are
available to which kind

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.920
of consumers, what
kind of quotas on them.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.320
And we can attach
additional data to it

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.690
that we can use to
make other decisions.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:37.050
We want to do those same sorts
of things with GraphQL APIs.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:43.070
So if you look at what
we've added to Apigee,

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:46.850
Apigee now has a way,
inside any Apigee proxy,

00:13:46.850 --> 00:13:49.490
to parse a GraphQL request.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.370
First of all, just to reject
it if it's invalid, or even

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.660
do schema validation.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:55.520
That can be super
valuable, because it

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:59.720
means that if you have malicious
users or developers who

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:02.330
are still learning the
API and making mistakes,

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:08.150
Apigee can nip all of
those invalid requests

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.740
right in the Apigee
proxy, and not

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.560
even let them get to your
back-end server, which takes

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.620
a load off your GraphQL engine.

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:17.690
But Apigee can now
parse that query,

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:22.950
understand what it's trying
to do, and make decisions.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:25.800
So a very simple thing you
could do, for instance,

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.460
is say that certain developers
or certain applications

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:30.200
are only allowed
to execute queries

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.288
against the GraphQL API.

00:14:32.288 --> 00:14:34.580
Whereas you have to have a
different kind of credential

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.500
and, possibly,
approval from the API

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:39.020
team in order to make mutations.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.060
Which means, basically,
we can very easily

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:45.500
separate the read only API
from the read or write API.

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:46.793
And then, we can do more.

00:14:46.793 --> 00:14:48.710
And I think what we're
doing in the future is,

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:50.910
we're looking at
what more we can do.

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.780
So for instance, we also,
now that we understand

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:56.750
the shape of the API, we can
put a bunch of information

00:14:56.750 --> 00:14:58.610
into the Apigee message flow.

00:14:58.610 --> 00:15:01.980
We can then use other policies
to make other decisions.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.100
So it gives a lot of
flexibility for you

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:09.290
as a developer, right now, to
build some interesting GraphQL

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:12.230
API-related access control
and other decision-making

00:15:12.230 --> 00:15:14.293
logic into your Apigee proxy.

00:15:14.293 --> 00:15:15.710
And it's going to
make it possible

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:18.120
for us to do even more
with this in the future.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.120
DAVID FEUER: And Greg,
do you see this affecting

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:22.370
how enterprises are
able to position

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:24.140
GraphQL APIs for consumption?

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:25.640
Like, is this
something that you see

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:29.540
helping application developers
build new experiences using

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:30.770
GraphQL APIs?

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:31.610
GREGORY BRAIL: Yeah.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:32.960
So if you're an application--

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:35.450
I mean, you and I have
been around the world

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:36.860
a little while.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:40.670
Once upon a time, it was
considered terribly bad form

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.850
to expose your relational
database directly

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:44.990
to developers.

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:46.910
Because developers
might be able to do

00:15:46.910 --> 00:15:50.360
something like select
star from customers--

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:53.300
or worse yet, delete
star from customers--

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:57.350
and overwhelm the database, or
execute some ridiculous query

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:00.860
that is nested 17 levels deep
and joins 12 different tables

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:03.710
together and slows down the
application for everybody.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:06.350
So that's where three-tier
computing and transaction

00:16:06.350 --> 00:16:09.120
processing monitors and
all these things came in.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:10.400
Same thing with GraphQL.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:14.450
If you just put a GraphQL API
in front of your core data

00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:17.810
and give access to developers
to get at it directly,

00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:20.270
then you have to have some
trust in your developers

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:22.790
that they're not going
to execute queries that

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:26.870
cause performance problems or
security problems or compliance

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:27.990
problems.

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:31.250
So if you have something sitting
in front of that GraphQL API

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:32.990
that's able to
look at the queries

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:36.440
and make decisions about what's
allowed and what's not allowed,

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:38.570
I think it makes it
easier for an enterprise

00:16:38.570 --> 00:16:44.200
to be OK with the idea of using
GraphQL for critical data.

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:46.565
DAVID FEUER: Awesome.

00:16:46.565 --> 00:16:48.440
GREGORY BRAIL: And with
that, we have a demo.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.030
You're going to listen to me.

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:51.530
And what we're going
to do is, we're

00:16:51.530 --> 00:16:54.530
going to show some of the new
GraphQL features of Apigee

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:56.213
and what we can do with them.

00:16:56.213 --> 00:16:56.880
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:59.870
- I'm going to start with an
example of a mobile application

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.790
that uses GraphQL.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:04.520
This application is
a product catalog.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:08.480
And it makes one API call to
get the product information,

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:13.859
plus an API call for every price
and every stock information.

00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:16.940
So as you can see, when
I refresh the screen,

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:19.010
we have a lot of
these blue circles

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:21.420
that show API calls being made.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.720
And when I click on
an individual product,

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:27.470
we make three API calls
to get the result.

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:30.140
However, I can also
switch my application

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:32.210
to take advantage of GraphQL.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:35.480
In that case, it makes one
API call to my back end.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:38.240
It's querying the exact
same database tables.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.930
In my case, it's even
calling the exact same gRPCs.

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:45.170
But the mobile app is sending
one request to the back end.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.120
And the back end is
fanning all of that

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:49.140
and returning one response.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:52.140
The result is quite a
bit more responsive.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.220
There's only one API call
required to get all the product

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:56.660
information I need.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:59.630
When I scroll up and
down, it's nice and zippy.

00:17:59.630 --> 00:18:02.370
And there's none of that
refreshing going on.

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:05.960
So as you can see, GraphQL
can be a very effective way

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:10.120
to make an efficient
and snappy mobile app.

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:11.590
Now that we've
looked at the app,

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.960
let's actually see what's
going on with the API

00:18:13.960 --> 00:18:15.370
under the covers.

00:18:15.370 --> 00:18:18.490
Underneath, of course,
I have a GraphQL API.

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:20.500
And I can do some
things in this GraphQL

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:23.350
API, like, for instance,
got a list of products.

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:25.450
And like in all
good GraphQL APIs,

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:27.700
I can merge data from
different data sets.

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:28.390
I can sort.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:29.980
I can decide how many.

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.680
And I can get information
about an individual product.

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:35.950
And once again, I can
decide exactly what fields

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:37.630
I want to see or don't see.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:41.140
And on this particular case,
this particular GraphQL query

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:43.840
is being secured
via an API key which

00:18:43.840 --> 00:18:46.170
is being enforced by Apigee.

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:49.410
Using this particular
API key, I can also

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.080
try and get information
about the price of an item.

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:56.550
And if I use that key, here's
my query to get the price.

00:18:56.550 --> 00:18:58.140
You'll see that I get an error.

00:18:58.140 --> 00:18:59.640
This is coming from Apigee.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:01.470
Because Apigee parsed the query.

00:19:01.470 --> 00:19:03.210
It saw that the
top-level selection

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:04.530
set was called "price."

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:08.730
And this particular API key is
not allowed to make that query.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:11.700
However, if I change
the API key to an API

00:19:11.700 --> 00:19:14.430
key that uses a
different API product,

00:19:14.430 --> 00:19:17.827
I now can get the price.

00:19:17.827 --> 00:19:19.410
Now that we've seen
with the API looks

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.960
like, let's see how
it works in Apigee.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.840
First of all, Apigee has
always had API products.

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:27.810
They're key to API management,
because they let you package up

00:19:27.810 --> 00:19:31.530
an API without changing the back
end into different combinations

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.170
of functionality,
different quota settings,

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:36.690
and even different
aspects, like security,

00:19:36.690 --> 00:19:39.090
depending on who's
consuming the API.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.340
We've added GraphQL
support to API products.

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:46.170
So now, in addition to filtering
on URL paths and verbs,

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:48.120
like we could
before, we can also

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:50.760
filter on, for instance,
the GraphQL operation type,

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:52.680
like whether it's a
query or mutation.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:55.140
And we can set different
quotas for different products,

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:56.280
like we always could.

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:57.930
So for instance,
you could set it up

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.990
so a certain API key or a
certain set of applications

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:04.110
that use oAuth might be
allowed only to do queries.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.420
Or they might have a different
quota than another set.

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:08.220
And they might
even be restricted

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.490
based on the name of
the GraphQL operation.

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:14.760
Now, the way this is enforced
is in the proxy, of course.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:18.030
And we have the first part
of functionality to do this,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:20.280
is our new GraphQL policy.

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:23.610
First thing it does is parse the
query to make sure it's valid.

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:26.310
It can also validate the
schema if you'd like.

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:28.050
And then, it sets a
bunch of variables

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.770
that we can use
for other things.

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:32.610
For instance, it's used
in the CheckAPIKey policy

00:20:32.610 --> 00:20:36.360
to enforce the API product
logic that I just talked about.

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:38.730
I wanted to go a little
bit further in this demo

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:40.800
and use the name of the
selection set, which

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:42.930
is the top of that
query, to determine

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:46.600
which API keys could query which
parts of my GraphQL schema.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.900
And I did that using
some JavaScript.

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:50.970
I set a variable
in the product that

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.570
specifies what top-level
selection sets are allowed.

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:58.860
And I use a variable that is
set by the GraphQL policy which

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:00.900
contains that information.

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:03.180
After that, a little
bit of error checking

00:21:03.180 --> 00:21:07.260
follows, and some clever
but simple JavaScript

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:10.650
that simply looks to see if
the name of that selection set

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:11.820
is in the product.

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:13.000
That's all there is to it.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.810
And now I'm able to have some
very interesting controls

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.340
on my GraphQL application.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:19.365
[END PLAYBACK]

00:21:19.365 --> 00:21:20.740
GREGORY BRAIL: So
we've just seen

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.830
a demo that shows how you
can use the new GraphQL

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:25.960
features of Apigee
to parse queries

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:28.630
and understand them, and
have additional logic

00:21:28.630 --> 00:21:31.480
to make decisions about
those to make your API more

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:32.950
ready for the enterprise.

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:34.707
Over to you, Dave.

00:21:34.707 --> 00:21:35.790
DAVID FEUER: Thanks, Greg.

00:21:35.790 --> 00:21:37.230
That was awesome.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:39.990
We're super excited about
GraphQL and our ability

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.960
to bring it to our customers
and prospects to build new apps

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:45.390
and experience with
this new technology.

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:48.310
Please go to
cloud.google.com/apigee to try

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:48.810
it out.

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.778
We'd love to hear
what you think.

00:21:50.778 --> 00:21:52.320
Thanks for joining
us today, and have

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:54.650
a great rest of the summit.

